  "deviations": [
    {
      "symbol": "yaml_parse()",
      "php": "an alias inside the node its anchor names builds a recursive array of references",
      "reason": "Rust values cannot be recursive: such an alias is refused as unknown (false)"
    },
    {
      "symbol": "yaml_parse()",
      "php": "ext-yaml corrupts its heap when a block mapping under an explicit key (`?`) fails to parse, in a mapping that already dropped a collection key (`[]: b\\n? 7: @`, `? [a]\\n: b\\n? q: r\\n  a: - x`: ext-yaml warns 'Illegal offset type array', then 'Unexpected event type 0' on libyaml's error and 'Illegal offset type array' again for the broken key) or in a document that defined an anchor when the key's value is an unknown alias (`m: &m v\\n? q: *x\\n- b`). yaml_parse() returns false, and the process dies later with 'zend_mm_heap corrupted', or the corruption shows in later parses (CI seed 10814 died on the input right after `? [a]\\n: b\\n? q: r\\n  '07':  -  0`).",
      "reason": "Memory corruption is undefined behaviour with no answer to compare; Rust refuses every such document (false, as for any malformed document or unknown alias). The fuzz profile never leaves a block mapping open under an explicit key: the composable lines use flow keys (`? {q: r}`, `n: &n 1\\n? {q: *n}`, `? [a]\\n: b`, `? q\\n: v`), which a following line cannot extend, and block-mapping keys appear only in whole, well-formed documents (`? [a]\\n: b\\n? q: r\\n  s: t\\n: v`, `n: &n 1\\n? q: *n\\n: v`)."
    }
  ]
